For every natural $n,N$ and rational $\varepsilon$ satisfying the parameter condition, the stated initial and transition properties hold. For a natural number $n$ (zero is allowed unless explicitly excluded), let $C_n=\{(i,j):0\le i<n,\ j\in\{0,1\},\ i+j<n\}$. The finite vertex set $V_n$ has distinct vertices $a_i,b_i$ for $i<n$, $c_{ijk},d_{ijk}$ for $(i,j)\in C_n$ and $k\in\{0,1\}$, $u_{ij},h_{ij},r_{ij}$ for $(i,j)\in C_n$, and $z$. These denote entry, selector, cycle, escape, upper, centre, reward, and sink vertices, respectively. The symbols $a_i,b_i,u_{ij},h_{ij},r_{ij},c_{ijk}$ are extended to all natural indices by returning $z$ whenever their displayed vertex indices are invalid; this is the exact fallback of the corresponding indexed vertex functions. Put $D_{ijk}=d_{ijk}$ when $c_{ijk}$ is a cycle vertex, and $D_{ijk}=z$ otherwise. The successor sets are $S(a_i)=\{b_i,a_{i+1}\}$; $S(b_i)=\{h_{i0},h_{i1}\}$ if $i+1<n$ and $\{h_{i0}\}$ otherwise; $S(c_{ijk})=\{h_{ij},d_{ijk}\}$; $S(d_{ijk})=\{a_1,b_0\}$; $S(u_{ij})=\{r_{ij},a_0\}$; $S(h_{ij})=\{c_{ij0},c_{ij1},u_{ij}\}$; $S(r_{ij})=\{a_{i+2}\}$ when $j=0$ and $\{b_{i+1}\}$ when $j=1$; and $S(z)=\{z\}$, with repetitions in a successor set counted once. A strategy is any total map $\sigma:V_n	o V_n$, an edge label is any ordered pair in $E_n=V_n	imes V_n$ whether or not it is a graph edge, and $\sigma$ is legal precisely when $\sigma(v)\in S(v)$ for every vertex other than $h_{ij},r_{ij},z$; there is no legality constraint on its values at those three vertex kinds. Write $O(v)$ for the Boolean which is true exactly at those constrained vertices. The priority function is $p(a_i)=p(c_{ijk})=p(d_{ijk})=3$, $p(b_i)=11+2i$, $p(u_{ij})=10-2j$, $p(h_{ij})=6-2j$, $p(r_{ij})=12+2i$, and $p(z)=1$, where the subtractions are natural-number subtractions. The initial strategy $\iota$ sends $a_i$ to $a_{i+1}$, $b_i$ to $h_{i0}$, $c_{ijk}$ to $d_{ijk}$, $d_{ijk}$ to $a_1$, and $u_{ij}$ to $r_{ij}$ for $j=0$ and to $a_0$ for $j=1$; it fixes every $h_{ij},r_{ij},z$. The parameter condition means exactly $n,N\in\mathbb N$, $\varepsilon\in\mathbb Q$, $0<n$, $7n\le N$, and $0<\varepsilon<(N^{2n+11})^{-1}$, with the power and inverse taken in the rationals. For any natural $N$ and rational $\varepsilon$, the MDP reward vector is $ho_N(b_i)=(-N)^{11+2i}$, $ho_N(u_{ij})=(-N)^{10-2j}$, $ho_N(r_{ij})=(-N)^{12+2i}$, and zero at all other vertices. Its decision predicate is true at every vertex except centre vertices $h_{ij}$ and $z$, so reward vertices are decisions even though legality of $\sigma$ does not constrain them. The rational transition matrix $T_{\varepsilon,\sigma}$ sends $h_{ij}$ to $u_{ij}$ with coefficient $\varepsilon$ and to each of $c_{ij0},c_{ij1}$ with coefficient $(1-\varepsilon)/2$, with zero coefficients elsewhere in that row; sends $r_{ij}$ with coefficient $1$ to its sole member of $S(r_{ij})$ and with coefficient $0$ elsewhere, independently of $\sigma(r_{ij})$; sends $z$ to itself with coefficient $1$; and, from every other vertex $v$, has coefficient $1$ exactly at $\sigma(v)$ and $0$ elsewhere. These formulas are defined for all rational $\varepsilon$ and all total $\sigma$, without an implicit probability or legality hypothesis. The policy matrix $M_{\varepsilon,\sigma}$ has the row $\mathbf1_{w=z}$ at $v=z$ and entries $\mathbf1_{v=w}-T_{\varepsilon,\sigma}(v,w)$ at every $v
